    "## 10. Summary of Results\n\n### Key Findings\n\n**Indicator 1 \u2014 Distance to Nearest POE**\n\n| Subarea | n | Median (mi) | Mean (mi) |\n|---------|---|-------------|-----------|\n| Otay Mesa | 466 | 1.06 | *see v10 output* |\n| Kearny Mesa | *see v10* | *see v10* | *see v10* |\n| Miramar | *see v10* | *see v10* | *see v10* |\n| Sorrento Valley | *see v10* | *see v10* | *see v10* |\n\n**Statistical result:** Mann-Whitney U, Otay Mesa vs all non-border \u2014 rank-biserial r = **0.972**, p < 0.0001.  \nA randomly selected Otay Mesa business is closer to a POE than a non-border business **97.2% of the time**.\n\n**Indicator 2 \u2014 Freight Corridor Buffer Overlap**\n\n| Buffer | Otay Mesa | Kearny Mesa | Miramar | Sorrento Valley |\n|--------|-----------|-------------|---------|-----------------|\n| 0.25 mi | 45.9% | *see v10* | *see v10* | *see v10* |\n| 0.50 mi | 84.5% | *see v10* | *see v10* | *see v10* |\n| 1.00 mi | 96.4% | *see v10* | *see v10* | *see v10* |\n\n**Core finding:** POE proximity is the dominant spatial signal distinguishing Otay Mesa's industrial activity. Corridor buffer overlap differences are statistically significant but modest in effect size (Cram\u00e9r's V = 0.17\u20130.28), reflecting that major freight roads serve industrial areas throughout San Diego County \u2014 not exclusively near the border.\n"
