    "## 11. Discussion\n\n### Findings in Context of Literature\nThe strong POE-proximity clustering documented here is consistent with research on border-zone industrial development showing that freight-oriented businesses self-select for locations minimizing cross-border transport costs. The near-complete separation (r = 0.972) is stronger than typical distance-decay effects found in urban industrial location literature, suggesting that the border crossing itself \u2014 not just road access \u2014 is the organizing principle of Otay Mesa's industrial geography.\n\nThe modest Cram\u00e9r's V values for corridor buffer overlap (0.17\u20130.28) are informative rather than disappointing: major freight roads serve industrial areas throughout San Diego County, so road proximity alone does not distinguish Otay Mesa. It is specifically the POE that marks Otay Mesa as distinctive.\n\n### Trade-offs and Decision Points\n\n**Buffer distance:** The choice of 0.25, 0.50, and 1.00 mile thresholds is researcher-defined. Prof. Zaslavsky's feedback noted that findings should not depend on one arbitrary distance \u2014 our three-threshold approach addresses this by showing consistency across distances.\n\n**CRS selection:** EPSG:3310 (California Albers) was chosen to match Christian's pipeline and uses meters as the distance unit, appropriate for the scale of analysis. Earlier versions used EPSG:2230 (US survey feet) \u2014 the switch to 3310 aligns all three sub-analyses.\n\n**Subarea definition:** Planning district boundaries replace earlier researcher-defined bounding boxes and 3-mile circles, grounding subarea definitions in official city geography and matching Christian's approach exactly.\n\n**Geocoding validity:** Geocode scores reflect address-matching confidence, not operational location accuracy. A business registered at a downtown office may operate its freight facility in Otay Mesa. This is the most significant unresolved methodological concern, as noted by Prof. Zaslavsky.\n\n**Sorrento Valley:** No official Sorrento Valley planning district exists. MIRA MESA was used as the closest available match. This should be noted when comparing results to Canyu's tract-level analysis, which uses a researcher-defined 3-mile circle.\n\n**Industrial zoning vs. business activity:** San Diego's industrial zoning reflects \"leftover land\" allocation (ZAPP analysis), not deliberate freight-access planning. This is why business license records \u2014 not zone polygons \u2014 are used as the primary study unit.\n"
