    "## 12. Conclusions & Future Work\n\n### Did We Answer the Research Question?\nYes, with important caveats. The spatial association between Otay Mesa's industrial activity and port of entry proximity is statistically significant and extremely strong (r = 0.972, p < 0.0001). However, the analysis documents where businesses are *registered*, not necessarily where their freight operations occur. The finding should be interpreted as: Otay Mesa is a distinctive industrial cluster organized around port proximity at the registration-address level, with the operational reality likely even more concentrated near the POEs.\n\n### Future Work\n\n1. **Border-logistics indicator businesses**  \n   Identify NAICS subcategories unique to or disproportionately concentrated in Otay Mesa \u2014 customs brokers (541614), freight forwarders (488510), cold storage (493120) \u2014 to construct a \"border-economy index\" as suggested by Prof. Zaslavsky. This would move beyond treating all industrial NAICS codes as equivalent.\n\n2. **Operational location validation**  \n   Validate a sample of geocoded businesses against known operational addresses (company websites, satellite imagery) to assess how often registered and operational addresses diverge. If divergence is systematic, it would affect the interpretation of distance indicators.\n\n3. **Sensitivity analysis on buffer distances**  \n   Test a wider range of buffer thresholds (0.1, 0.5, 1, 2, 5 miles) to confirm that findings are not sensitive to the chosen distances.\n\n4. **Tijuana-side analysis**  \n   Extend to the Mexican side of the border. Maquiladora zones directly across from Otay Mesa represent the other half of the cross-border production system not captured in this analysis.\n\n5. **Drive-time distance**  \n   Replace straight-line POE distance with network/drive-time distance accounting for road geometry and border wait times \u2014 a more operationally meaningful measure of freight access.\n\n6. **Otay Mesa East POE**  \n   Model the expected spatial shift in industrial clustering if the proposed new crossing opens, using the distance-decay relationship documented here.\n\n7. **LODES employment data**  \n   Use Census LODES to distinguish where workers are *employed* (vs. where they *reside*) at the tract level, separating production workers from commuters in Canyu's ACS-based analysis.\n\n### Expected Use\nResults are most immediately relevant to SANDAG regional planners and City of San Diego economic development staff considering land use decisions near the proposed Otay Mesa East crossing. The spatial framework developed here could be extended to other US\u2013Mexico border crossings in California and Texas. Connor Jennings (industry professional, cc'd by Prof. Zaslavsky) may have specific suggestions about project extensions and real-world application contexts.\n\n---\n*Notebook prepared for GPEC 447, UC San Diego, Spring 2026.*\n"
